\begin{abstract}
Fine-tuned to allocate 50\% of attention to system-prompt goals (GAR\,=\,0.50 under teacher forcing), Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct retains only GAR\,=\,0.064 under autoregressive generation---an 8$\times$ collapse. We trace this failure to exposure bias: attention patterns optimized under teacher-forced prefixes do not transfer to model-generated text. ThreadGate, per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gates trained with an auxiliary GAR loss, provides a controlled testbed. A five-way comparison isolates turn conditioning, gating, gradient conflict, and fine-tuning; each pair kills one hypothesis. Gating outperforms LoRA-only ($p$\,=\,0.004) but the gap remains 7.8$\times$; PCGrad eliminates gradient conflict but \emph{worsens} AR GAR ($p$\,=\,0.002), ruling out the most intuitive failure mechanism by \emph{modus tollens}. Steering ($\beta$\,=\,5) recovers GAR to 0.727; scheduled sampling compresses the gap to 1.44$\times$ (single seed). Cross-model evaluation on Llama-3.1-8B confirms a 27$\times$ gap. These findings establish that exposure bias selectively destroys auxiliary attention losses while NTP self-recovers---a failure mode with no prior multi-turn transformer characterization.
\end{abstract}

